      {
        "tipo": "intro",
        "min": 3,
        "texto": "Picture the scene: you arrive in a square in Rome and two friends meet right next to you. In a few seconds you will hear Italian for the first time. You don't need to understand everything. Just listen to the melody. You already know a lot of Italian: pizza, pasta, espresso, cappuccino, gelato, piano, opera, ciao. Italian is written almost exactly as it is spoken, and that helps a lot. But some letter combinations follow sound rules, and that is what this lesson teaches. Next to the words there is an approximate pronunciation in English-style spelling. Capital letters mark the stressed syllable. «ch» is the sound in «church». «j» is the sound in «jet». «ny» is the sound in «canyon» and «ly» the sound in «million». «sh» is the sound in «shoe». «g» and «k» are always hard. Every vowel is pure: «ah», «eh», «ee», «oh», «oo», never glided. It is a guide, not a replacement for the audio: always listen before you repeat."
      },
