        "titulo": "When to use each greeting",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «ciao» is the greeting for friends and family. It means hi and also bye. STEP 2: «buongiorno» works in the morning and into the early afternoon. STEP 3: «buonasera» works from late afternoon onwards, when you arrive somewhere. STEP 4: to leave politely, say «arrivederci». It works with everyone. STEP 5: «grazie» says thank you and «prego» answers it. It means «you're welcome». When someone offers you something, «prego» also means «go ahead, help yourself». STEP 6: «scusi» asks a stranger for permission or says sorry. Between friends, you say «scusa». STEP 7: «per favore» is «please». Culture: in Italy, when you walk into a bar or a shop you say «buongiorno» to the people there. Walking in silently sounds rude.",
        "comparacao": "in English «hi» and «bye» work with anyone. In Italian, «ciao» is only for people you know well. With strangers, use «buongiorno», «buonasera» and «arrivederci».",
